# Servers that can write are HTTPS only

Any server that can change data is reachable only over HTTPS: the gRPC annotation service, the section-correction
service, Identity, and any other web server or gRPC service with a write path. Bearer tokens and annotation data must
never cross the network in plain text.

- **No plain listener on the host.** Compose files publish only the TLS port (`443` in the container). Do not publish
  an `h2c` / HTTP port (e.g. annotation `5010:80`, section correction `41080:80`), not even "for tests" or bound to
  `0.0.0.0`.
- **Test stacks and local runs use TLS too.** With no real certificate the container uses its self-signed
  `localhost` certificate; tests and clients call `https://localhost:<port>` and trust that certificate explicitly.
  Do not fall back to `http://` because a certificate is missing.
- **Production uses a real certificate** from the single viking-certs renewer (`docker-letsencrypt-sidecar` skill),
  normally behind the viking-proxy on 443.
- **Read-only, non-data endpoints** (a static demo page, ACME HTTP-01 challenges on the certificate renewer) may use
  HTTP only when they cannot write anything and carry no token; say why in a comment next to the port.
- **Clients** build `https://` endpoints. Do not add code that rewrites an `https` endpoint to `http`, or that accepts
  any certificate.

Existing plain ports (annotation `5010`, section correction `41080`, and the h2c test stack that uses them) predate
this rule and are to be removed, not copied.
